Hi everyone,

I am struggling with some model I am testing using Nimble. In particular one of the parameters is not sampled.

My model aims to assess fish migration dynamics (salmon migrating towards the sea) by taking into account the effect of capture on migration dynamics and trap efficiency ("p_MDP").

During their migration, the fish can be caught in the 1st trap (Lesle). They are then marked and release and some of them are then recaptured at a 2nd trap (MDP) further downstream. However, they do not necessarily continue their migration immediately after initial capture and tagging at Trap 1, as some fish will continue to migrate after one day only, while others may take several days. So the day of recapture at trap 2 will depend on the day they migrated after capture/marking at trap 1 and the probability of capture at trap 2.

To represent this, I built a model using a dirichlet-multinomial distribution, using the function from the nimble manual (https://r-nimble.org/html_manual/cha-user-defined.html#sec:user-distributions ). I separated explicitly the migration process from the capture process.

Specifically, I estimate the probability of migration for each day after the first capture over 10 days/10 time steps with an exponential distribution. The 10th time step is the number of fishes captured at 10 days or more after the marking.

for (i2 in 1:Nm) {# calcul of the migration probability for each marking day (i2) and each time steps (1 to 10)

      p_migr[i2, 1] <- 1-exp(-0.25*lambda[i2, 1])

      for(d in 2:9){

        p_migr[i2, d] <- (1-sum(p_migr[i2, 1:(d-1)]))*(1-exp(-0.25*lambda[i2, d] - 0.75*lambda[i2, d - 1]))

      }

      p_migr[i2, 10] <- 1 - sum(p_migr[i2, 1:9])

    }

    for (i4 in 1:Nm) { #calcul of the alpha for the Dirichlet multinomial distribution

      for(d in 1:10){

      alpha[i4, d] <- p_migr[i4, d] * n_p_migr

      }

    }

    for (i5 in 1:Nm) {  #redistribution or the migrating salmon thanks to a Dirichlet multinomial distribution

      n_migr_lesle[i5, i5:(i5 + 9)] ~ ddirchmulti(alpha = alpha[i5, 1:10], size = n_lesle[i5]) #n_lesle: number of fish marked the day i5

    }

    for (i6 in 1:(Nm - 10)) {#for each marking day

      for (j in i6:(i6 + 8)) {#recapture time step

        n_lesle_mdp[i6, j] ~ dbinom(p_MDP[j], n_migr_lesle[i6, j])

      }

      p_MDP_mean[i6] <- sum(p_MDP[(i6 + 9):Nm])/(Nm-(i6 + 9)+1)

      n_lesle_mdp[i6, i6 + 9] ~ dbinom(p_MDP_mean[i6], n_migr_lesle[i6, i6 + 9])

    }

    for (i7 in ((Nm - 10) + 1):Nm) {#last time steps

      for (j in i7:Nm) {#sur les jours de recaptures

        n_lesle_mdp[i7, j] ~ dbinom(p_MDP[j], n_migr_lesle[i7, j])

      }

    }

But the sample size of dirichlet-multinomial distribution is not sampled and stay at the initial value. I tried different initialization values. As it didn’t work, I made a new model, without the latent decomposition (“n_migr_lesle”), with the probability p = probability of migration x probability of capture at trap 2, and sample size “n_p_migr”, which finally worked for each year separately but not when I combined 6 years of data in a hierarchical model.

You can find data and model attached:

