Hi Chris,
I think I understood where is my error. The problem is, my "observation matrix" should not be a matrix but a whole distribution and I am not quite sure how to do this ? Because I think about two ways to do it : (1) discretize my speed by producing an observation matrix base only on the observations in my dataset ? But by doing so, every line of my Observation matrix would not sum up to 1 OR (2) not having an observation matrix and computing my likelihood directly on a Gamma distribution for each observation "y" with either S or NS parameter according to the state "z". Is one of these two methods a way to do it ?

Rémy C.

Le mardi 25 juillet 2023 à 17:43:34 UTC+2, Chris Paciorek a écrit :
> Hi Rémy,
>
> Internally, NIMBLE reparameterizes this gamma distribution:
>
> y[i,t] ~ dgamma(shape = omega[z[i,t], 1],
>                       rate = omega[z[i,t], 2])
>
> using the inverse of the rate parametr. That introduces a new parameter for the inverse rate (the scale parameter) named `lifted_d1_over_omega_oBz_oBi_comma_t_cB_comma_2_cB_L30`.
> The message suggests that `omega` is not being correctly initialized, which causes the new parameter also not to be initialized.
>
> From your code it looks like you are initializing things, so my suggestion is to build the model using `nimbleModel` (separately from using `nimbleMCMC`, so that you can explore what is going on), and then look at the values in the model:
>
> model$omega
> model$z
>
> As far as your question about initializing 'z', yes, you can initialize with some random values.
>
> -chris
>
> On Tue, Jul 25, 2023 at 2:22 AM Rémy C. <remy....@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Hi Chris,
> > Thank you so much for your anwsers !
> > So if I understand it well, I can initialize z with some random values (1 or 2) and it should converge afterwards ?
> >
> > zinits <- matrix(sample(c(1, 2), my.constants$T, replace = TRUE),
> >                  nrow = 1, byrow = TRUE)
> >
> > That's what I tried (see above) and I am moving forward, I managed to debug some parametrization issues, but there is one I am still struggling with. When I try to create the model, I get this warning :
> > mod.output$initializeInfo()  [Note] Missing values (NAs) or non-finite values were found in model variables: lifted_d1_over_omega_oBz_oBi_comma_t_cB_comma_2_cB_L30.
> > Is it still my initialization of the z matrix which is wrong ?
> > Le lundi 24 juillet 2023 à 18:30:27 UTC+2, Chris Paciorek a écrit :
> > > Hi Rémy,
> > >
> > > All I was saying was that the initial z values have to be legitimate in terms of being valid index values. I'm not sure I'd call them 'fake', but rather 'initial' states.
> > >
> > > I am not looking at your context closely enough to be able to say anything about two distributions (speed and turning angles). All I'm saying is that if you have an unknown index in the model, the initial value for that index needs to be a valid integer-valued index.
> > >
> > > -chris
> > >
> > > On Mon, Jul 24, 2023 at 1:09 AM Rémy C. <remy....@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > Hi Chris and thank you very much for your anwser !
> > > > So if I understand well, I'll have to make the states according to two distributions (i.e. speed and turning angles) rather than with a z matrix of 2x2 ? But I have some trouble figuring out the exact way to do this (knowing that the states are unkown). Do I have to initialise the z matrix with random "fake" states ?
> > > >
> > > > Rémy C.
> > > >
> > > >
> > > > Le dimanche 23 juillet 2023 à 22:07:06 UTC+2, Chris Paciorek a écrit :
> > > > > Hi Rémy,
> > > > >
> > > > > The message indicates that the initial values for `z` are in some way not valid indices for `gamma` and `omega`. Your initial values for `z` need to be either 1 or 2 because `gamma` and `omega` have only two rows. But you are initializing `z` (which must be 1 or 2) the same as the initial values of `y` (with y being continuous random variables), so that is presumably the cause of the problem.
> > > > >
> > > > > -Chris
> > > > >
> > > > > On Fri, Jul 21, 2023 at 5:50 AM Rémy C. <remy....@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > > > Hello everyone,
> > > > > > I am fairly new to nimble and I am trying to make my first model in order to estimate if my fish is more in a "foraging" state or in-transit. This states depends on the distance travelled every day (and associated turning angles, but I'll add complexcity when I'll figure how this works exactly) which is associated with a gamma distribution.
> > > > > >  My problem is that : when I try to run everything (with only one invidividual, always to keep it simple at start) I have this weird error :
> > > > > >
> > > > > > "Building model Setting data and initial values Running calculate on model [Note] Any error reports that follow may simply reflect missing values in model variables.  [Warning] Dynamic index out of bounds: dcat(prob = gamma[z[i, t_minus_1], 1:2]) [Warning] Dynamic index out of bounds: dcat(prob = gamma[z[i, t_minus_1], 1:2]) [Warning] Dynamic index out of bounds: 1/omega[z[i, t], 2]"
> > > > > >
> > > > > > Here is my code :
> > > > > >
> > > > > > '''
