Your anwser works much better than what I tried ! Thank you ! But We aim to test for a third state (but I wanted to start easier) so I had to go back to the speed matrix.
While I tried your method, it still talked about parameters which are not initialized. I read NAs were considered as non data but ass soon as I sampled only non-NA data in my data it worked pretty well. (see the error below)(1)
"warning: logProb of data node y[128, 2029]: logProb is -Inf. warning: logProb of data node y[128, 2047]: logProb is -Inf. warning: logProb of data node y[128, 2051]: logProb is -Inf."

I am so sorry, but I'd have another (and last I hope ! But nimble is not as simple as it seemed for someone who never did bayesian [fault on me for this one !]) question. Getting back with my speed matrix, the "lifted ...." parameter still seemed to be wrongly initialized. But when I tak a closer look, the model produces one vector of "lifted..." for the first part of the likelihood (for the first data observed) and one matrix for the remaining observations but with the first column full of NA ... I tried to initialize the speed matrix which didn't work as well... Would you have an idea on how to "merge" the vector for it to fit as the first column of the other matrix ?

Here is my brand new code for those who'd be interested :

hmm.traj <- nimbleCode({

  # Prior
  ## Transition
  phi.S ~ dunif(0, 1)
  phi.NS ~ dunif(0, 1)

  ## Speed

  mean.S ~ dunif(5, 40)  # Prior shape Gamma distribution (distance) : in-search

  sd.S ~ dunif(0.01, 50)   # Prior scale Gamma distribution (distance) : in-search

  mean.NS ~ dunif(40, 100) # Prior shape Gamma distribution (distance) : in-transit

  sd.NS ~ dunif(0.01, 50)  # Prior scale Gamma distribution (distance) : in-transit

  ## Initial state probability
  delta[1] <- 0.5
  delta[2] <- 0.5

  # Transition matrix
  gamma[1,1] <- phi.S      # Pr(in-search t -> in-search t+1)
  gamma[1,2] <- 1 - phi.S  # Pr(in-search t -> in-transit t+1)
  gamma[2,1] <- 1 - phi.NS # Pr(in-transit t -> in-search t+1)
  gamma[2,2] <- phi.NS     # Pr(in-transit t -> in-transit t+1)

  # Speed matrix
  speed[1,1] <- mean.S
  speed[1,2] <- sd.S
  speed[2,1] <- mean.NS
  speed[2,2] <- sd.NS

  # Likelihood
  ## Iteration over individuals
  for (i in 1:N) {

    z[i,first[i]] ~ dcat(delta[1:2])

    y[i, first[i]] ~ dgamma(mean = speed[z[i,first[i]],1], sd = speed[z[i,first[i]],2])

    ### Iteration over time
    for (t in (first[i]+1):last[i]) {

      z[i,t] ~ dcat(gamma[z[i,t-1],1:2])

      y[i, t] ~ dgamma(mean = speed[z[i,t],1], sd = speed[z[i,t],2])

    } # t
  } # i

})

```

Get the date of first location.
```{r}
first <- apply(y, 1, function(x) min(which(!is.na <http://is.na>(x))))
last <- apply(y, 1, function(x) max(which(!is.na <http://is.na>(x))))
```

Specification of the constants and the data
```{r}
(my.constants <- list(N = nrow(y),
                      first = first,
                      last = last))

my.data <- list(y = y)

```

Let's write a function for the initial values
```{r}

z.init <- y
for (i in 1:nrow(y)) {
  for (j in first[i]:ncol(y)) {
    if (!is.na <http://is.na>(y[i,j])){
    z.init[i,j] <- rcat(1, c(0.5,0.5))
    }
  }
}

mean.init <- z.init
sd.init <- z.init
for (i in 1:nrow(z.init)) {
  for (j in first[i]:last[i]) {
    if (z.init[i,j] == 1){
      mean.init[i,j] <- 30
      sd.init[i,j] <- 5
    } else if (z.init[i,j] == 2){
      mean.init[i,j] <- 60
      sd.init[i,j] <- 5

    }
  }
}

initial.values <- function() list(phi.S = runif(1, 0, 1),
                                  phi.NS = runif(1, 0, 1),

                                  mean.S = 30,
                                  mean.NS = 60,
                                  sd.S = 5,
                                  sd.NS = 5,
                                  z = z.init,
                                  curr.mean = mean.init,
                                  curr.sd <http://curr.sd> = sd.init)
```

We specify the parameters we'd like to monitor
```{r}

(parameters.to.save <- c("phi.S", "phi.NS",

                        "mean.S", "mean.NS",
                        "sd.S", "sd.NS"))
```

We try the model
```{r}
mod.traj <- nimbleModel(code = hmm.traj,

                          constants = my.constants,
                          data = my.data,

                          inits = initial.values())

